# LabLink Competition Engineering Rules

Build LabLink, an AI-powered university laboratory resource-sharing platform.

The platform must support:
- Cross-department equipment discovery
- Intelligent equipment matching
- Project-based access requests
- Slot booking
- Faculty approvals
- Fair conflict resolution
- Deadline-aware scheduling
- Resource utilization optimization

## Architecture

Frontend:
- React
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS

Backend:
- Python
- FastAPI
- Google Cloud Run

Data:
- Firebase Authentication
- Cloud Firestore

AI:
- Gemini for project understanding and conversational reasoning
- RAG for manuals, equipment documents and safety policies
- Live structured data must be used for availability and booking

Google Cloud:
- Cloud Run
- Firebase
- Cloud Storage where required
- Secret Manager where required

## Critical rules

- Do not use AI to make final booking or access-control decisions.
- Deterministic code must enforce eligibility and availability.
- Use transactions or equivalent concurrency-safe mechanisms for booking.
- Never double-book resources.
- Never expose secrets in frontend code.
- Never trust client-provided user roles.
- Validate all inputs.
- Enforce authorization on the backend.
- Use grounded citations for RAG responses.
- Never fabricate equipment, policies, availability or approval status.
- Prevent booking restricted equipment without verified prerequisites.
- Explain fairness-based allocation decisions.
- Keep faculty approval in the loop for contested or restricted requests.

## Quality

- Maintainable code.
- Typed interfaces.
- Minimal dependencies.
- Proper error handling.
- Unit tests.
- Integration tests for booking and scheduling.
- Accessibility.
- Responsive design.
- No dead buttons.
- No fake functionality.
- No hardcoded metrics that pretend to be live.
- No unnecessary features.

## Competition constraints

- Public repository.
- Exactly one branch.
- Repository under 10 MB.
- Cloud Run deployment.
- Publicly accessible deployment.
- No secrets in GitHub.
- Clean README.
- Verify production before submission.

## Development workflow

- Read and understand all requirements.
- Plan architecture before implementation.
- Implement core functionality first.
- Test all critical flows.
- Audit all six scoring criteria.
- Deploy.
- Test production.
- Only then prepare submission.

Do not overengineer.
Prioritize a complete, reliable, demonstrable solution.
